      title: "Islamic Banking: Business versus Benevolence",
      overview: "The author dispels the myth that Islamic banks should function as charities/social-security institutions, clarifying that Islamic banks are businesses with fiduciary duties to depositors, while benevolence remains a separate, optional activity funded differently.",
      simpleExplanation: "Some people think Islamic banks should mainly give out free (interest-free, no-strings) loans as charity. That's a myth the author wants to correct: business and benevolence/charity are two separate things. Individual people can and should be charitable with their own money, and get spiritual reward for it — but a bank holds other people's (depositors') money as a trust, and can't just give that money away at its own discretion. Since it's usually middle-class savers whose money funds business activity for wealthier entrepreneurs, the bank's job is to do real, profitable, Shari'ah-compliant business (trading, leasing, profit-sharing) and pass income back to depositors — charitable activities can only come from the bank's own equity funds or a separate accumulated Charity Fund, with Shari'ah-board approval, and shouldn't compromise the bank's core duty to depositors.",
      academicExplanation: "The author identifies and dispels a specific myth: that Islamic banks should function as social-security centres, providing mainly return-free loans/charity to the needy. He insists business and benevolence are two separate things — individuals may spend for benevolence from their own income (rewarded in the Hereafter per Shari'ah), but banks holding depositors' money as a trust cannot dole out those trust funds at their own discretion. He notes the typical funding-flow pattern: the 'middle class' across societies generally keeps funds in banks, which are then used by business groups who are generally more affluent/richer than the masses — Islamic banks do business with these mobilized funds and pass a portion of the resulting income back to fund owners (depositors/investors). A bank may choose to provide return-free loans specifically from its own equity funds or an accumulated 'Charity Fund' (with Shari'ah advisor approval), or engage in other social-security activities, but this must not compromise its fiduciary responsibilities toward depositors. To meet these fiduciary duties, banks conduct trading and Ijarah business, provide agency-based fee services, and apply risk-mitigation techniques within Shari'ah limits. In sum: Islamic banks sell goods they've purchased at a profit, lease assets for rentals, and share profit/bear loss from Shirkah-based investments — thereby helping society by facilitating asset-based investment and the supply of risk-based capital; they may participate in social/welfare activities subject to board policy and stakeholder consultation, but this is not, and should not become, their normal course of business.",
      examExplanation: "Myth dispelled: Islamic banks are NOT meant to function as charities/social-security institutions providing free loans as their core business. Business and benevolence are separate: individuals may give charity from their own income, but banks hold depositors' money as a trust and cannot dole it out at will. Charitable/return-free-loan activity by a bank must come specifically from the bank's own equity funds or a separate accumulated Charity Fund (with Shari'ah board approval) — never from depositor funds meant for the bank's fiduciary business activities. Islamic banks' actual business: selling purchased goods at a profit, leasing assets for rentals, sharing Shirkah-based profit/loss.",
